# ============================================================
#  Caddyfile — hostname-routed local dev.
#
#  Each service gets its own name instead of a path prefix:
#
#      https://api.sakajob.home:7443     backend
#      https://n8n.sakajob.home:7443     n8n
#
#  There is no monitoring vhost: this host reports into a Beszel hub owned by
#  another project, so only the agent runs here.
#
#  Why names instead of /api and /n8n: an app hosted under a path prefix has to
#  rewrite every absolute URL it emits, and n8n is fiddly about it. On its own
#  hostname each app is simply at "/" and none of that applies.
#
#  Ports 7080/7443 rather than 80/443: another stack on this machine binds
#  0.0.0.0:80 and :443. Docker Desktop does not honour per-loopback-IP binds
#  (a `-p 127.0.0.2:443` request still tries to allocate 0.0.0.0:443 and
#  fails), so a distinct port pair is the only option. Caddy listens on the
#  SAME port numbers inside the container as outside, which keeps redirects,
#  certificate SANs, and Host matching all consistent.
#
#  Requires these lines in the hosts file (see infra/README.md):
#      127.0.0.1  api.sakajob.home
#      127.0.0.1  n8n.sakajob.home
#      127.0.0.1  hub.sakajob.home
#
#  For production: replace the .home names with real domains, drop the :7443
#  suffixes and the `tls internal` lines, and map 80/443. Caddy then fetches
#  real Let's Encrypt certificates automatically.
# ============================================================

{
	# Local dev: issue certificates from Caddy's own CA.
	# (Remove this whole block in production.)
	local_certs
}

# Shared response hardening + compression, applied to every site below.
(common) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000;"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Referrer-Policy "no-referrer"
		-Server
	}
	encode gzip
}

# --- Backend API ---
# The path convention differs from the localhost fallback below: here the API
# is at the root (https://api.sakajob.home:7443/jobs), because there is no
# prefix to strip.
https://api.sakajob.home:7443 {
	tls internal
	import common
	reverse_proxy backend:2623
}

# --- n8n ---
https://n8n.sakajob.home:7443 {
	tls internal
	import common
	reverse_proxy n8n:5678
}

# --- localhost fallbacks ---
# The hostnames above are the intended way in. These stay so that a machine
# without the hosts-file entries still has a route to every service, and so
# the documented curl examples keep working.
#
# The API keeps its /api/* prefix here: it serves JSON and never emits an
# absolute asset path, so a prefix costs it nothing.
https://localhost:7443 {
	tls internal
	import common

	handle_path /api/* {
		reverse_proxy backend:2623
	}

	respond "JobHunter edge OK — use https://api.sakajob.home:7443 (n8n: n8n., monitoring: hub.)" 200
}

# n8n additionally gets its own port, because it does not survive a path prefix:
# it builds absolute asset URLs from the site root, so under /n8n/ the HTML loads
# while every /static/* request falls through to the block above and returns the
# banner instead of JavaScript. A dedicated port puts it at "/" with no
# rewriting. Caddy is still the only thing exposed to the host.
https://localhost:7444 {
	tls internal
	import common
	reverse_proxy n8n:5678
}


# --- plain HTTP redirects to HTTPS ---
http://api.sakajob.home:7080, http://n8n.sakajob.home:7080, http://localhost:7080 {
	redir https://{host}:7443{uri} permanent
}

# ============================================================
#  Plain-HTTP back ends for `tailscale serve`
# ============================================================
#
# Tailscale terminates TLS itself, with a real Let's Encrypt certificate for
# the machine's *.ts.net name, and forwards plaintext to a local port. So these
# blocks are HTTP on purpose: adding `tls internal` would make Caddy present
# its own CA's certificate to tailscaled, which would reject it — and the whole
# reason for using Tailscale here is to get a certificate the Flutter app will
# actually accept.
#
# Two details that are easy to get wrong:
#
#   1. The site address is a bare `:port`, NOT `http://localhost:port`. Caddy
#      matches on the Host header, and tailscaled forwards with the .ts.net
#      hostname — a `localhost` site block would never match and every request
#      would 404.
#
#   2. These ports are published on 127.0.0.1 ONLY (see docker-compose.yml).
#      They carry no TLS, so binding them to 0.0.0.0 would put the API in
#      cleartext on whatever café wifi the laptop is joined to. Loopback means
#      only the host's own tailscaled can reach them.
#
# The API at the root, so the app's base URL is just https://<machine>.ts.net.
:7081 {
	import common
	reverse_proxy backend:2623
}

# n8n on its own port for the same reason it has one locally: it builds
# absolute asset URLs from the site root and does not survive a path prefix.
# Front it with a second `tailscale serve --https=8443` if you want the editor
# reachable away from home.
:7082 {
	import common
	reverse_proxy n8n:5678
}
